{
  "image_name": "0bc4a602f2f4340251dd2b1a720137c3.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how loss of the checkpoint kinases CHK1 or WEE1 affects DNA replication, contrasting a simple “CDK overactivation” model with a more adaptive one. At the top, CHK1- and WEE1-proficient cells maintain normal CDK activity, controlled nuclease activity, stable replication forks, and a balanced dNTP supply between repair and replication, producing normal fork speed and origin firing.\n\nBelow, CHK1- or WEE1-deficient cells are shown in two possible outcomes. In the canonical model, excess CDK activity increases replication initiation across all cells, which eventually exhausts limiting factors, slows forks, and causes DNA damage. The replication adaptation model is more nuanced. In normal and immortalized cells, CDK overactivation also triggers unscheduled nuclease activity, but ATM pathway activation and redistribution of dNTPs toward repair partially compensate, leading only to moderate fork slowing. In contrast, U2OS cancer cells with p16/p19 deficiency experience stronger stress: a short G1 phase leaves dNTP pools low, forks slow more severely, compensation is inadequate, and RPA becomes limiting. Overall, the diagram argues that replication stress after CHK1 or WEE1 loss depends not just on excess origin firing, but on how cells adapt their nucleotide economy and fork protection.",
